<article class="env-lemma" id="05ZY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05ZY">Lemma <span data-tag="05ZY">76.14.3</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. If $f : X \to Y$ is a formally unramified morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$, then given any solid commutative diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X \ar[d]_ f &  T \ar[d]^ i \ar[l] \\  Y &  T' \ar[l] \ar@{-->}[lu] }  \]
</div>
  <p> where $T \subset T'$ is a first order thickening of algebraic spaces over $S$ there exists at most one dotted arrow making the diagram commute. In other words, in Definition <a href="/tag/04G7" data-tag="04G7">76.14.1</a> the condition that $T$ be an affine scheme may be dropped. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is true because there exists a surjective étale morphism $U' \to T'$ where $U'$ is a disjoint union of affine schemes (see Properties of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/03FX" data-tag="03FX">66.6.1</a>) and a morphism $T' \to X$ is determined by its restriction to $U'$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>